/T (The Inequality Working Group continued to explore how algorithms, markets, and public institutions shape access to resources and opportunities, with a particular focus on social welfare programs, labor markets, and participatory research. Throughout the semester, discussions examined both the promises and pitfalls of data-driven decision-making in contexts where the stakes are highest for vulnerable populations.)
/E (The Inequality Working Group continued to explore how algorithms, markets, and public institutions shape access to resources and opportunities, with a particular focus on social welfare programs, labor markets, and participatory research. Throughout the semester, discussions examined both the promises and pitfalls of data-driven decision-making in contexts where the stakes are highest for vulnerable populations.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
559 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 323 0 R
/T (Highlights included Sera Linardi's discussion of participatory and community-engaged research methods, Unai Fischer Abaigar's presentation of The Value of Prediction in Identifying the Worst-Off, and Angela Zhou's work Due Process on Hold: A Queueing Framework for Improving Access in SNAP. The group also welcomed Nikhil Garg, who presented Heterogeneous Allocation Skews: When is Choice "Worth It?", and the authors of Two Tickets are Better than One: Fair and Accurate Hiring under Strategic LLM Manipulations, sparking discussion about the growing role of AI in labor markets. Additional sessions explored mechanism design and social programs through Zi Yang Kang's Optimal In-Kind Redistribution and examined algorithmic decision-making in welfare systems through a case study of bias-aware fraud detection in Amsterdam.)
/E (Highlights included Sera Linardi's discussion of participatory and community-engaged research methods, Unai Fischer Abaigar's presentation of The Value of Prediction in Identifying the Worst-Off, and Angela Zhou's work Due Process on Hold: A Queueing Framework for Improving Access in SNAP. The group also welcomed Nikhil Garg, who presented Heterogeneous Allocation Skews: When is Choice "Worth It?", and the authors of Two Tickets are Better than One: Fair and Accurate Hiring under Strategic LLM Manipulations, sparking discussion about the growing role of AI in labor markets. Additional sessions explored mechanism design and social programs through Zi Yang Kang's Optimal In-Kind Redistribution and examined algorithmic decision-making in welfare systems through a case study of bias-aware fraud detection in Amsterdam.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2 890 0 R 4 891 0 R 6 892 0 R 8 893 0 R 10]
