<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 328 0 R
/E (As part of the Spring 2026 EAAMO Colloquium Series, Rachel Franklin \(Harvard University Center for Geographic Analysis\) presented Spatial Inequality and the Smart City, examining how data-driven urban systems can both illuminate and reinforce existing inequalities. The talk explored the relationship between emerging smart city technologies, spatial data, and urban decision-making, highlighting the ways in which the collection and use of data are shaped by geography, infrastructure, and unequal access to resources. Franklin encouraged participants to consider not only what data are available, but also whose experiences and communities may be missing from the data used to guide policy and planning.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
641 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 328 0 R
/E (Drawing on examples from urban analytics and transportation research, the discussion focused on how spatial inequalities can become embedded in ostensibly objective technological systems. The talk emphasized the importance of designing data collection, measurement, and governance processes that account for uneven coverage and representation, particularly for historically underserved populations. By bringing together perspectives from geography, urban studies, and data science, the colloquium highlighted both the opportunities and challenges of using computational tools to create more equitable and responsive cities.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
