659 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 329 0 R
/E (As part of the Spring 2026 EAAMO Colloquium Series, Juba Ziani \(Georgia Tech\) and Sanmay Das \(Virginia Tech\) presented Fixed Points and Stochastic Meritocracies: A Long-Term Perspective, exploring how inequalities can emerge and persist even in systems that appear fair at any given point in time. Using the example of merit-based selection processes such as college admissions, the speakers examined how feedback loops across generations can shape long-term outcomes, raising important questions about the limits of traditional fairness frameworks. Their work highlighted how disparities may arise not only from explicit bias, but also from chance, scarcity, and the cumulative effects of past advantages.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
660 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 329 0 R
/E (The colloquium focused on the dynamic nature of fairness, emphasizing that equal treatment in the present does not necessarily lead to equitable outcomes in the future. Through theoretical analysis and simulations, Ziani and Das demonstrated how small, initially random differences between groups can persist and even become entrenched over time, particularly when access to valuable opportunities is limited. The discussion underscored the importance of studying fairness as a long-term process and offered new insights into the design of algorithmic systems and policies intended to promote equity in education, employment, and other domains involving competitive selection.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
