/Title (The Urban Data Science Working Group continued to examine how data, computation, and policy intersect in the design and governance of cities. This semester featured a series of talks exploring transportation equity, educational access, and spatial inequality through data-driven research and policy analysis.)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2639 0 R
/Next 2650 0 R
>>
endobj
2644 0 obj
<<
/Title (Together, these sessions highlighted the potential of urban data science to better understand and address structural inequalities, reinforcing the working group's commitment to advancing inclusive and evidence-based approaches to urban challenges.)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2639 0 R
/Prev 2650 0 R
>>
endobj
2645 0 obj
<<
/Title (The Inequality Working Group continued to explore how algorithms, markets, and public institutions shape access to resources and opportunities, with a particular focus on social welfare programs, labor markets, and participatory research. Throughout the semester, discussions examined both the promises and pitfalls of data-driven decision-making in contexts where the stakes are highest for vulnerable populations.)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2640 0 R
/Next 2646 0 R
>>
endobj
2646 0 obj
<<
/Title (Highlights included Sera Linardi's discussion of participatory and community-engaged research methods, Unai Fischer Abaigar's presentation of The Value of Prediction in Identifying the Worst-Off, and Angela Zhou's work Due Process on Hold: A Queueing Framework for Improving Access in SNAP. The group also welcomed Nikhil Garg, who presented Heterogeneous Allocation Skews: When is Choice "Worth It?", and the authors of Two Tickets are Better than One: Fair and Accurate Hiring under Strategic LLM Manipulations, sparking discussion about the growing role of AI in labor markets. Additional sessions explored mechanism design and social programs through Zi Yang Kang's Optimal In-Kind Redistribution and examined algorithmic decision-making in welfare systems through a case study of bias-aware fraud detection in Amsterdam.)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2640 0 R
/Prev 2645 0 R
>>
endobj
